1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:03,428
Enron wasn't destroyed
because it ran out of customers.

2
00:00:03,998 --> 00:00:07,383
In 2001, its pipelines were
still moving gas.

3
00:00:07,403 --> 00:00:09,533
Its traders were still making deals.

4
00:00:09,803 --> 00:00:12,878
The real problem had
started almost a decade earlier,

5
00:00:12,898 --> 00:00:15,272
with a decision about how to count money.

6
00:00:15,542 --> 00:00:19,832
In 1992, Enron got permission
to use an accounting

7
00:00:19,852 --> 00:00:22,855
method that could turn
profits expected in the future...

8
00:00:22,875 --> 00:00:24,422
into profits reported today.

9
00:00:24,692 --> 00:00:26,458
The cash didn't have to arrive yet.

10
00:00:26,478 --> 00:00:29,264
But on paper, Enron could
look spectacular.

11
00:00:29,884 --> 00:00:32,604
And as Wall Street
fell in love with the company,

12
00:00:32,624 --> 00:00:35,008
keeping that picture alive
became critical.

13
00:00:35,278 --> 00:00:38,973
By 2000, Enron was worth
nearly seventy billion dollars.

14
00:00:39,243 --> 00:00:41,258
Then, in less than
two months, it was gone.

15
00:00:42,128 --> 00:00:44,696
Billions in savings. Thousands of jobs.

16
00:00:44,716 --> 00:00:47,351
One of America's most admired companies.

17
00:00:47,621 --> 00:00:51,673
To understand how, you need one
accounting idea that changed everything.

18
00:00:54,993 --> 00:00:57,237
Houston, Texas. 1985.

19
00:00:57,507 --> 00:01:01,607
Two natural gas pipeline companies merge
into what soon becomes Enron,

20
00:01:01,627 --> 00:01:03,165
led by Kenneth Lay.

21
00:01:03,435 --> 00:01:05,825
At first, Enron moves gas. Steady.

22
00:01:05,845 --> 00:01:07,344
Predictable. A little boring.

23
00:01:07,614 --> 00:01:11,527
Then Lay brings in a sharp McKinsey
consultant named Jeffrey Skilling.

24
00:01:11,797 --> 00:01:16,227
Skilling's big idea was a "Gas
Bank." Enron wouldn't just move energy.

25
00:01:16,247 --> 00:01:18,778
It would trade it, the way a
bank trades money.

26
00:01:19,048 --> 00:01:19,567
It worked.

27
00:01:19,587 --> 00:01:23,017
By the late nineties, Enron was
trading natural gas,

28
00:01:23,037 --> 00:01:25,012
electricity, even internet bandwidth.

29
00:01:25,282 --> 00:01:30,180
Fortune magazine named it "America's
Most Innovative Company" six years in a row.

30
00:01:30,450 --> 00:01:35,670
In 2000, Enron reported revenue
of about one hundred billion dollars.

31
00:01:35,690 --> 00:01:38,260
That August, its
stock hit an all-time high.

32
00:01:38,280 --> 00:01:40,203
More than ninety dollars a share.

33
00:01:40,473 --> 00:01:41,794
Enron looked like the future.

34
00:01:42,064 --> 00:01:45,816
But underneath that success was
a question almost nobody was asking.

35
00:01:46,086 --> 00:01:48,891
How much of that profit
had actually arrived as cash?

36
00:01:51,261 --> 00:01:54,135
To answer that,
we have to go back to 1992.

37
00:01:54,405 --> 00:01:57,991
Skilling wanted Enron
to use mark-to-market accounting.

38
00:01:58,011 --> 00:02:01,759
In January 1992, the Securities
and Exchange Commission

39
00:02:01,779 --> 00:02:04,881
approved it for Enron's
natural gas contracts.

40
00:02:05,151 --> 00:02:08,473
Imagine Enron signs
a ten-year contract it expects

41
00:02:08,493 --> 00:02:10,952
to earn one hundred million
dollars in profit.

42
00:02:11,222 --> 00:02:14,749
Under traditional accounting, that
profit shows up gradually,

43
00:02:14,769 --> 00:02:17,482
as the gas is delivered and
the money comes in.

44
00:02:17,752 --> 00:02:22,702
Under mark-to-market, Enron could estimate
what the whole contract was worth today,

45
00:02:22,722 --> 00:02:24,481
and book that value as profit now.

46
00:02:24,751 --> 00:02:26,374
Year one. One giant number.

47
00:02:27,194 --> 00:02:29,112
Now, mark-to-market isn't fraud.

48
00:02:29,132 --> 00:02:32,323
It's widely used for
things with clear market prices.

49
00:02:32,593 --> 00:02:34,697
The danger is in one word. Estimate.

50
00:02:35,417 --> 00:02:39,577
Many of Enron's long-term deals had no
market price to check against.

51
00:02:39,597 --> 00:02:42,570
Their value rested
on Enron's own assumptions.

52
00:02:42,840 --> 00:02:47,029
Make those assumptions a little more
optimistic, and the profit gets bigger.

53
00:02:47,049 --> 00:02:47,583
Instantly.

54
00:02:47,853 --> 00:02:49,524
And here's the crucial part.

55
00:02:49,544 --> 00:02:51,155
The profit is reported now.

56
00:02:51,175 --> 00:02:53,209
The cash might arrive years later.

57
00:02:53,229 --> 00:02:55,323
Or never match the estimate at all.

58
00:02:56,043 --> 00:02:59,548
Accounting profit is what the
financial statements say you earned.

59
00:02:59,568 --> 00:03:02,752
Cash flow is the money that
actually comes through the door.

60
00:03:03,022 --> 00:03:05,015
At Enron, the two drifted far apart.

61
00:03:05,285 --> 00:03:06,228
One real example:

62
00:03:06,248 --> 00:03:09,795
in July 2000, Enron
announced a twenty-year

63
00:03:09,815 --> 00:03:12,230
deal with Blockbuster for
movies on demand.

64
00:03:12,500 --> 00:03:17,181
It never got beyond small pilot tests,
and it ended in March 2001.

65
00:03:17,451 --> 00:03:20,611
But Enron had already booked
about one hundred

66
00:03:20,631 --> 00:03:23,078
and eleven million dollars
in profit from it,

67
00:03:23,098 --> 00:03:25,435
based largely on projected
future earnings.

68
00:03:26,355 --> 00:03:29,645
And once you've reported
a contract's future profit today,

69
00:03:29,665 --> 00:03:31,643
you can't report it again tomorrow.

70
00:03:31,913 --> 00:03:34,870
So to keep growing, Enron
needed more deals.

71
00:03:34,890 --> 00:03:36,730
Bigger deals. Every single quarter.

72
00:03:37,000 --> 00:03:38,882
The accounting had built a treadmill.

73
00:03:38,902 --> 00:03:40,065
And it was speeding up.

74
00:03:42,435 --> 00:03:46,446
In 1999, Enron's stock rose
fifty-six percent.

75
00:03:46,466 --> 00:03:48,951
In 2000, another eighty-seven percent.

76
00:03:49,221 --> 00:03:52,624
Stock tickers were installed in
the lobbies and elevators.

77
00:03:52,644 --> 00:03:55,339
Executives were paid heavily
in stock options.

78
00:03:55,609 --> 00:03:57,539
The share price wasn't just a number.

79
00:03:57,559 --> 00:03:58,699
It was the scoreboard.

80
00:03:58,969 --> 00:04:02,425
Analysts expected Enron
to hit its targets every quarter.

81
00:04:02,445 --> 00:04:04,944
And Enron delivered. Again. And again.

82
00:04:05,214 --> 00:04:07,754
But every success raised the
bar for the next one.

83
00:04:08,024 --> 00:04:09,877
Meanwhile, some of its biggest bets,

84
00:04:09,897 --> 00:04:13,415
like overseas power plants and
a broadband network, were struggling.

85
00:04:13,685 --> 00:04:15,627
The machine needed constant growth.

86
00:04:15,647 --> 00:04:18,037
And real growth
was getting harder to find.

87
00:04:18,307 --> 00:04:21,836
So where do you put the losses, and the debt,
when your story can't afford them?

88
00:04:24,206 --> 00:04:28,662
Enter Andrew Fastow, Enron's chief
financial officer from 1998.

89
00:04:28,932 --> 00:04:31,089
His specialty: special-purpose entities.

90
00:04:31,359 --> 00:04:35,085
Basically, a separate company
created for one specific job.

91
00:04:35,105 --> 00:04:37,942
Companies use them
legitimately all the time.

92
00:04:38,212 --> 00:04:42,187
Under the rules back then, an
outside investor with as little as three

93
00:04:42,207 --> 00:04:46,411
percent of the capital at risk could make
the entity count as independent.

94
00:04:46,681 --> 00:04:47,492
And that mattered.

95
00:04:47,512 --> 00:04:51,326
Because its assets, and its debt, could
stay off Enron's balance sheet.

96
00:04:51,596 --> 00:04:55,254
Enron used them again and again. Chewco.

97
00:04:55,274 --> 00:04:55,488
LJM.

98
00:04:55,508 --> 00:04:58,991
And the Raptors, named after the
dinosaurs in Jurassic Park.

99
00:04:59,261 --> 00:05:02,818
But in several cases, the
outside money wasn't really outside.

100
00:05:03,088 --> 00:05:06,634
Chewco, it later emerged, didn't meet
the three percent rule at all.

101
00:05:07,254 --> 00:05:12,019
The Raptors made Enron's investment
losses look hedged, covered by someone else.

102
00:05:12,039 --> 00:05:16,075
But that someone else was effectively
backed by Enron's own stock.

103
00:05:16,345 --> 00:05:20,867
A later investigation for Enron's
board found these deals inflated

104
00:05:20,887 --> 00:05:24,885
reported earnings by almost one billion
dollars, in just over a year.

105
00:05:25,805 --> 00:05:27,601
Then there was the conflict of interest.

106
00:05:27,871 --> 00:05:31,215
Fastow didn't just
design the LJM partnerships.

107
00:05:31,235 --> 00:05:33,268
He ran them, while serving as C.F.O.

108
00:05:33,288 --> 00:05:37,943
Enron's board approved it, as
an exception to its own code of conduct.

109
00:05:38,213 --> 00:05:42,736
The investigation found Fastow
was involved on both sides of these deals,

110
00:05:42,756 --> 00:05:45,516
and made more than thirty
million dollars from them.

111
00:05:46,336 --> 00:05:48,525
On paper, Enron looked stronger than ever.

112
00:05:48,795 --> 00:05:52,616
In reality, risk was piling up
in places investors could barely see.

113
00:05:54,986 --> 00:05:56,629
But some people started looking.

114
00:05:56,899 --> 00:05:58,089
March 2001.

115
00:05:58,109 --> 00:06:02,142
In Fortune, the same magazine
that kept calling Enron innovative,

116
00:06:02,162 --> 00:06:04,405
reporter Bethany McLean asked:

117
00:06:04,425 --> 00:06:06,194
"Is Enron Overpriced?"

118
00:06:07,014 --> 00:06:07,877
The real question:

119
00:06:07,897 --> 00:06:10,329
how exactly does Enron make its money?

120
00:06:10,599 --> 00:06:13,881
The financial statements
were nearly impossible to decode.

121
00:06:13,901 --> 00:06:15,076
The debt was growing.

122
00:06:15,096 --> 00:06:18,093
And the cash didn't seem
to line up with the profits.

123
00:06:18,363 --> 00:06:21,318
In August, just six
months after becoming C.E.O.,

124
00:06:21,338 --> 00:06:24,415
Jeffrey Skilling resigned,
citing personal reasons.

125
00:06:24,685 --> 00:06:27,933
Kenneth Lay took the job back,
and assured the market:

126
00:06:27,953 --> 00:06:29,715
no change in performance or outlook.

127
00:06:29,985 --> 00:06:34,852
The next day, a vice president named
Sherron Watkins sent Lay an anonymous letter.

128
00:06:35,122 --> 00:06:36,622
One line would become famous.

129
00:06:37,342 --> 00:06:42,026
"I am incredibly nervous that we will implode
in a wave of accounting scandals."

130
00:06:43,196 --> 00:06:46,320
By the end of August, the
stock was below thirty-five dollars.

131
00:06:46,590 --> 00:06:48,330
The cracks were no longer invisible.

132
00:06:50,700 --> 00:06:52,636
October sixteenth, 2001.

133
00:06:52,906 --> 00:06:58,114
Enron reports a third-quarter loss
of six hundred and eighteen million dollars,

134
00:06:58,134 --> 00:07:00,642
after one point zero one
billion in charges.

135
00:07:00,912 --> 00:07:05,663
It also reveals a one point two
billion dollar cut to shareholders' equity,

136
00:07:05,683 --> 00:07:08,335
tied to partnerships run by its own C.F.O.

137
00:07:08,605 --> 00:07:10,644
October twenty-second. The S.E.C.

138
00:07:10,664 --> 00:07:12,142
starts asking questions.

139
00:07:12,162 --> 00:07:14,701
The stock falls to around twenty dollars.

140
00:07:14,971 --> 00:07:16,918
October twenty-fourth. Fastow is out.

141
00:07:17,188 --> 00:07:18,104
November eighth.

142
00:07:18,124 --> 00:07:22,841
Enron restates its results all
the way back to 1997.

143
00:07:22,861 --> 00:07:25,648
Hundreds of millions
in reported profit, erased.

144
00:07:25,668 --> 00:07:28,981
Hundreds of millions in
debt, suddenly back on the books.

145
00:07:29,251 --> 00:07:31,358
And a trading company runs on trust.

146
00:07:31,378 --> 00:07:33,485
Partners have to believe you'll pay.

147
00:07:33,755 --> 00:07:34,997
That trust was vanishing.

148
00:07:35,267 --> 00:07:36,543
November ninth. A lifeline.

149
00:07:36,563 --> 00:07:39,904
Dynegy, a Houston
rival, agrees to buy Enron.

150
00:07:40,174 --> 00:07:42,304
But the more Dynegy looked,
the worse it got.

151
00:07:42,574 --> 00:07:43,659
November twenty-eighth.

152
00:07:43,679 --> 00:07:45,870
Credit agencies cut Enron to junk.

153
00:07:45,890 --> 00:07:47,360
Dynegy walks away.

154
00:07:47,630 --> 00:07:50,319
That day, Enron's stock closes
at sixty-one cents.

155
00:07:51,389 --> 00:07:53,224
December second, 2001.

156
00:07:53,244 --> 00:07:56,771
Enron files for Chapter 11
bankruptcy protection.

157
00:07:57,041 --> 00:08:00,108
With sixty-three point
four billion dollars in assets,

158
00:08:00,128 --> 00:08:03,652
it was the largest bankruptcy
in American history at the time.

159
00:08:04,272 --> 00:08:07,579
From more than ninety dollars a
share, to under one dollar.

160
00:08:07,599 --> 00:08:08,933
In about fifteen months.

161
00:08:11,603 --> 00:08:13,812
But Enron was never just a stock chart.

162
00:08:14,632 --> 00:08:19,309
Within days of the filing, about four thousand
employees in Houston were laid off.

163
00:08:19,579 --> 00:08:22,719
Many had built their
retirement on Enron stock.

164
00:08:22,739 --> 00:08:27,058
It made up roughly sixty percent of
the company's 401(k) plan.

165
00:08:27,328 --> 00:08:31,034
Employees lost more than a
billion dollars in retirement savings.

166
00:08:31,854 --> 00:08:35,318
Investors lost tens of billions of
dollars in market value.

167
00:08:35,588 --> 00:08:38,807
And Enron's auditor, Arthur Andersen,

168
00:08:38,827 --> 00:08:41,918
one of the five biggest accounting firms
in the world, went down with it.

169
00:08:42,188 --> 00:08:45,121
Its staff had shredded Enron documents.

170
00:08:45,141 --> 00:08:49,464
In June 2002, the firm was convicted
of obstruction of justice,

171
00:08:49,484 --> 00:08:51,027
and its business collapsed.

172
00:08:51,297 --> 00:08:54,417
The Supreme Court later
overturned the conviction.

173
00:08:54,437 --> 00:08:56,490
By then, almost nothing was left.

174
00:08:57,410 --> 00:08:59,464
Fastow pleaded guilty.

175
00:08:59,484 --> 00:09:03,918
Skilling was convicted and sentenced to
twenty-four years, later reduced.

176
00:09:03,938 --> 00:09:06,968
Lay was convicted too,
but died before sentencing.

177
00:09:09,438 --> 00:09:11,056
So what really destroyed Enron?

178
00:09:11,976 --> 00:09:13,531
Not one accounting method.

179
00:09:13,551 --> 00:09:15,894
Not one partnership. Not one executive.

180
00:09:16,164 --> 00:09:17,425
It was a combination.

181
00:09:17,445 --> 00:09:21,390
Aggressive estimates that pulled
future profits into the present.

182
00:09:21,410 --> 00:09:25,050
Debt and losses moved where
investors could barely see them.

183
00:09:25,070 --> 00:09:27,185
A C.F.O. on both sides of the deal.

184
00:09:27,205 --> 00:09:29,808
Disclosures almost no one
could understand.

185
00:09:29,828 --> 00:09:33,041
And relentless pressure to keep
the numbers climbing.

186
00:09:33,311 --> 00:09:36,337
Mark-to-market didn't
destroy Enron on its own.

187
00:09:36,357 --> 00:09:39,176
But it helped open the
gap at the center of this story.

188
00:09:39,446 --> 00:09:43,160
The gap between looking profitable,
and actually generating cash.

189
00:09:44,230 --> 00:09:47,344
Enron spent years showing
Wall Street what it wanted to see.

190
00:09:47,614 --> 00:09:52,390
But accounting could only delay the
moment when expectations collided with reality.

191
00:09:52,660 --> 00:09:57,065
And once investors stopped believing
the numbers, Enron didn't have years left.

192
00:09:57,885 --> 00:09:58,778
It had weeks.
